WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.222
FULL OUTER JOIN preserves unmatched rows from both sides.

00:00:04.222 --> 00:00:11.037
It is especially useful when we are comparing two systems and want to see matches as well as

00:00:11.037 --> 00:00:12.967
differences. Look at the tiny tables.

00:00:12.967 --> 00:00:20.002
Ben has no admission, so the FULL OUTER result keeps Ben and fills the admission value with NULL.

00:00:20.002 --> 00:00:27.627
Admission 104 has no matching patient, so that admission also remains and the patient name becomes NULL.

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:30.097
Matched rows remain as usual.

00:00:30.097 --> 00:00:36.037
The PostgreSQL example uses a patient master table and an admissions staging table.

00:00:36.037 --> 00:00:39.182
This is a common data-quality situation.

00:00:39.182 --> 00:00:45.787
The patient master represents the official source, while the staging table represents imported data.

00:00:45.787 --> 00:00:50.677
We want to see records that match, patient records with no staging row,

00:00:50.677 --> 00:00:54.677
and staging rows whose patient ID does not exist in the master.

00:00:54.677 --> 00:00:59.462
The CASE expression translates those situations into readable labels.

00:00:59.462 --> 00:01:05.437
COALESCE helps us show one identifier even when the row exists on only one side.

00:01:05.437 --> 00:01:09.122
Think of FULL OUTER JOIN as a reconciliation tool.

00:01:09.122 --> 00:01:13.642
Finance teams use similar logic to compare transaction systems.

00:01:13.642 --> 00:01:18.177
Human resources teams may compare employee and payroll records.

00:01:18.177 --> 00:01:22.998
Data engineers use it to compare source and target tables during migrations.
